LIB-1 (2026-07-24 second audit, HIGH; reachability settled affirmative 2026-07-25): the wrapped-vector carrier equates computations SAW distinguishes. SAW's vectors are element-lazy (genOp builds delayed thunks; at forces only the selected one), so an error parked in an unread slot is never observed and A/B evaluate to 7/9 with A == B false. The Lean carrier Except String (Vec n T) cannot represent "error in one slot, good values elsewhere": genWithBoundsM sequences every element and short-circuits denotationally, so both terms collapse to Except.error "e" and every case observes error against a SAW value. The collapse is non-injective and lands on BOTH sides of emitted equations, so a SAW-false equation closes by rfl in a clean kernel — a carrier defect no replay gate can catch, because the proof itself is sound. Same class, unmeasured reach: genM, vecSequenceM, atRuntimeCheckedM, foldrM/foldlM, sawLet. Disposition (agreed 2026-07-25): interim (b) reject element bodies that can throw; successor (a) move Except inside the element (0.03-scale carrier change). This row is the reachability pin; it flips to a rejection pin when (b) lands.
